# sv_split_rvalue_receiver.z -- `split` on a view with no address of its own (a
# call's result) splits it.
#
# The split leg addressed its receiver as written, so `(s.substring ..).split`
# emitted `&` of a call and the C compiler refused it. The receiver is now bound
# to a temporary the call can address, as a view argument already was; the
# view owns nothing, so the temporary frees nothing. `count`, `lines`, `trim`
# and the parse members took such a receiver already, and are pinned beside it.

main: function is {
    sel: "@val&sum&x"
    for each t: ((sel.substring from: 1 to: sel.length).split sep: "&") loop print t
    with sp: ((sel.substring from: 5 to: sel.length).split sep: "&") do for u: sp loop print u
    print "count \{(sel.substring from: 1 to: 4).count}"
    for each l: (("a\nb".substring from: 0 to: 3).lines) loop print "line \{l}"
}
